From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri.3012@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Jun 2026 20:55:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] bcma: ssb: support the BCM4352/BCM4360 family

Everything the BCM4352/BCM4360 family needs from bcma, ssb and the
bcm47xx NVRAM parser, in one place. Each section below is one change,
with its reasons.

Add SPROM revision 11 extraction
--------------------------------

SPROM revision 11 is introduced with the BCM4352/BCM4360 family. Extend
struct ssb_sprom with the rev-11 fields actually parsed (per-band rxgains,
per-chain maxp/pa power info, 80 MHz MCS power offsets, pdoffset40ma)
and add bcma_sprom_extract_r11(), dispatched from bcma_sprom_get() when the
SPROM revision word reads as 11. bcma_sprom_get() already probes the rev-11
buffer size, and bcma_sprom_valid() populates bus->sprom.revision before
the dispatch. Revisions <= 10 keep the extract_r8 path.

The rev-11 field set and word offsets follow the public bcmdhd SROM
parser table (include/bcmsrom_tbl.h, GPLv2).

Header fields (IL0MAC/ANTAVAIL/TXRXC/CCODE and the ledbh0-3 LED
behaviours in gpio0-3) reuse the rev-8 masks/shifts; only the absolute
offsets moved. CCODE uses 0x96, since rev-8 0x92 collides with the middle
word of the rev-11 IL0MAC block. The LED words are 0x9A/0x9C: extract_r8
would read them from 0x96/0x98, which on rev 11 are CCODE and REGREV, and
leaving them unset gives b43 zeros instead of the 0xff that selects its
LED defaults.

Also extracts the explicit per-rate-group offsets of the bonded channels'
sub-band rows (sb20in40*, sb20in80and160*, sb40and80*, dot11agdup*,
mcslr5g*; SROM11 words 200-218 of bcmsrom_fmt.h) and decodes the antenna
gain (agbg0/aga0, whole dB in [5:0] plus quarter dB in [7:6]) into
antenna_gain_qdb[], which the regulatory ceiling subtracts from EIRP, and pdoffset80ma, the
per-chain power-detector offsets of the 80 MHz rate groups.

The five rpcal words, the TX beamforming RX path calibration for 2.4 GHz
and the four 5 GHz sub-bands, are words 182, 183, 190, 191 and 198,
between the per-rate words. Rev 11 has no 160 MHz per-rate field in the
SPROM: those words are the rpcal ones.

The temperature fields -- tempthresh, tempoffset, rawtempsense, measpower,
tempsense_slope, tempcorrx, tempsense_option, phycal_tempdelta,
temps_period and temps_hysteresis -- sit in rev 11 four bytes below their
rev-8 words with the same layout, so the SPROM8 masks apply. The
TG789vac v2 dump, the one board here that programs them, has THERMAL =
0x7800 and TEMPDELTA = 0x5500, matching tempthresh=120 and
temps_period=5 in its NVRAM.

PMU init (PLL + resources) for the BCM4352/BCM4360 family
---------------------------------------------------------

Neither bcma_pmu_pll_init() nor bcma_pmu_resources_init() have a case
for the BCM4352/BCM4360 family; both are no-ops for this chip today.

PLL: the stock driver writes PLLCTL2/PLLCTL3 unconditionally during
attach. Values captured from an MMIO trace of the stock driver's
attach sequence on a BCM43b3 (BCM4352-family) device (Netgear D6220,
wl-diag-d6220-4352-wl1-attach.txt, #161359-161360, last of three observed
attach cycles in the capture -- see commit message discussion for why
the earlier two are not used as-is). Only one crystal/board sample is
available, so this is not split by xtalfreq like the existing 43142
case; revisit if a board with a different crystal shows different
values.

Resources: program the PMU max resource mask to 0x7ff. This value was
read back at runtime from the ChipCommon PMU of a live BCM4360 and is
applied as-is; not yet confirmed on BCM4352 (D6220/DSL-3580L).

Add BCM4352 (0x43b3) to the PCI bridge table
--------------------------------------------

The BCM4352 is an AC PHY. It is missing from bcma_pci_bridge_tbl[],
so bcma-pci-bridge does not bind. Add the ID.

The chip identifies as BCM4352 (chip rev 3, PHY type AC); b43 with AC-PHY
support handles it once bcma reaches the D11 core.

Carry the NVRAM ledbh4..ledbh15 LED mappings
--------------------------------------------

The SPROM has fields for the LEDs on GPIO pins 0-3 only, ledbh0-3, and
struct ssb_sprom mirrors that as gpio0-3. Broadcom boards map LEDs on any
of the sixteen GPIO lines through NVRAM ledbh4..ledbh15, which the stock
driver reads with the same device prefix as ledbh0-3; on the Netgear D6220
and the D-Link DSL-3580L the 5 GHz activity LED is ledbh10=0x88.

On those boards the 802.11 core is a PCIe device with its own SPROM, so
bcma reads the SPROM from the device and the NVRAM fallback never runs:
whatever the board says in NVRAM about the device is lost. The stock
driver merges the two; do the same for the variables the SPROM has no word
for.

Add gpio_ext[12] for pins 4-15. bcm47xx_sprom fills it in two places: in
bcm47xx_sprom_fill_auto(), next to ledbh0-3, for a SPROM that comes from
NVRAM, and through bcm47xx_sprom_fill_bcma_nvram(), which resolves the
bus's prefix as the fallback does, for a SPROM read from the device; bcma
calls the latter after extracting the device SPROM. The prefix resolution
moves out of bcm47xx_get_sprom_bcma() into a helper the two share. Like
the fallback, this exists only with bcma built in; elsewhere the call is
an inline no-op.

Carry boardflags3 and AvVmid_c0..2 from NVRAM
---------------------------------------------

The AC-PHY programs each chain's power-detector input with a pair, Av and
Vmid, per band. A board gives the pairs in NVRAM as AvVmid_c0..2 -- ten
comma-separated values, Av,Vmid for 2g, 5gl, 5gml, 5gmu and 5gh, as in the
public Broadcom NVRAM files -- and the stock driver takes them from there
only when boardflags3 has BFL3_AvVim (0x40000000), "load AvVim from nvram"
in the same files; without the flag it uses a default set of its own.

AvVmid has no field in struct ssb_sprom; add avvmid[3][5][2] and
avvmid_valid, the chains whose variable parsed, so that a flag without the
variable leaves the driver on its defaults. boardflags3 has one, filled
from NVRAM by bcm47xx_sprom_fill_auto() for a SPROM that comes from NVRAM,
but nothing fills it for a SPROM read from the device: the rev 11
extraction does not take the word. Read both in
bcm47xx_sprom_fill_nvram_only(), next to ledbh4..15, which runs in either
case.

Signed-off-by: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri.3012@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/bcma/driver_chipcommon_pmu.c      |  42 +++++
 drivers/bcma/host_pci.c                   |   1 +
 drivers/bcma/sprom.c                      | 210 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/firmware/broadcom/bcm47xx_sprom.c | 124 ++++++++++---
 include/linux/bcm47xx_sprom.h             |  16 ++
 include/linux/ssb/ssb.h                   |  74 ++++++++
 include/linux/ssb/ssb_regs.h              | 110 ++++++++++++
 7 files changed, 554 insertions(+), 23 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/bcma/driver_chipcommon_pmu.c b/drivers/bcma/driver_chipcommon_pmu.c
index 263ef6f..cf93698 100644
--- a/drivers/bcma/driver_chipcommon_pmu.c
+++ b/drivers/bcma/driver_chipcommon_pmu.c
@@ -156,6 +156,32 @@ static void bcma_pmu_pll_init(struct bcma_drv_cc *cc)
 			xtalfreq = 20000;
 		bcma_pmu2_pll_init0(cc, xtalfreq);
 		break;
+	case BCMA_CHIP_ID_BCM4352:
+	case BCMA_CHIP_ID_BCM4360:
+		/*
+		 * PLLCTL2/PLLCTL3 on a cold attach, same values on both chips:
+		 * agcombo (4360) and D6220 (4352) both write 0x0c31 and 0x100e
+		 * with a full mask, then read them back later in the same
+		 * sequence (D6220 attach-to-bss-up #15, #23, then #164, #172).
+		 * A down->up capture only shows the reads, which is why these
+		 * writes were once thought to be 4360-only.
+		 *
+		 * The DSL-3580L, also a 4352, is a known exception: its stock
+		 * driver is missing the fractional vcofreq support (checked with
+		 * strings), leaves PLLCTL3 at the ROM value 0x00133333, and
+		 * works that way. Newer stock drivers do set vcofreq. Writing
+		 * these values there is therefore untested, not a fix.
+		 */
+		bcma_chipco_pll_write(cc, BCMA_CC_PMU_PLL_CTL2, 0x0c31);
+		bcma_chipco_pll_write(cc, BCMA_CC_PMU_PLL_CTL3, 0x100e);
+		/*
+		 * Latch them: the PMU only takes the new PLL control values on
+		 * PLL_UPD. The stock driver raises it immediately after these
+		 * two writes. Same flush bcma_pmu2_pll_init0() already does for
+		 * the PMU15 path.
+		 */
+		bcma_pmu_set32(cc, BCMA_CC_PMU_CTL, BCMA_CC_PMU_CTL_PLL_UPD);
+		break;
 	}
 }
 
@@ -188,11 +214,27 @@ static void bcma_pmu_resources_init(struct bcma_drv_cc *cc)
 			  BCMA_RES_4314_WL_CORE_READY;
 		max_msk = 0x3FFFFFFF;
 		break;
+	case BCMA_CHIP_ID_BCM4352:
+	case BCMA_CHIP_ID_BCM4360:
+		/*
+		 * Confirmed by reading the live PMU on both a BCM4360
+		 * (agcombo) and a BCM4352 (DSL-3580L): max_res_mask reads
+		 * back 0x7ff on hardware. min_res_mask is left untouched
+		 * (min_msk stays 0) as the ROM value (observed 0x7fb).
+		 */
+		max_msk = 0x7ff;
+		break;
 	default:
 		bcma_debug(bus, "PMU resource config unknown or not needed for device 0x%04X\n",
 			   bus->chipinfo.id);
 	}
 
+	bcma_debug(bus,
+		   "PMU res mask pre-write: min=0x%08x max=0x%08x (writing min=0x%08x max=0x%08x)\n",
+		   bcma_pmu_read32(cc, BCMA_CC_PMU_MINRES_MSK),
+		   bcma_pmu_read32(cc, BCMA_CC_PMU_MAXRES_MSK),
+		   min_msk, max_msk);
+
 	/* Set the resource masks. */
 	if (min_msk)
 		bcma_pmu_write32(cc, BCMA_CC_PMU_MINRES_MSK, min_msk);
diff --git a/drivers/bcma/host_pci.c b/drivers/bcma/host_pci.c
index 3dc2985..f6cf722 100644
--- a/drivers/bcma/host_pci.c
+++ b/drivers/bcma/host_pci.c
@@ -298,6 +298,7 @@ static const struct pci_device_id bcma_pci_bridge_tbl[] = {
 	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0x43a9) },
 	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0x43aa) },
 	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0x43b1) },
+	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0x43b3) },
 	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 0x4727) },
 	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 43227) },	/* 0xa8db, BCM43217 (sic!) */
 	{ PCI_DEVICE(PCI_VENDOR_ID_BROADCOM, 43228) },	/* 0xa8dc */
diff --git a/drivers/bcma/sprom.c b/drivers/bcma/sprom.c
index e668ad7..24852ad 100644
--- a/drivers/bcma/sprom.c
+++ b/drivers/bcma/sprom.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/bcma/bcma.h>
 #include <linux/bcma/bcma_regs.h>
+#include <linux/bcm47xx_sprom.h>
 #include <linux/pci.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
@@ -474,6 +475,205 @@ static void bcma_sprom_extract_r8(struct bcma_bus *bus, const u16 *sprom)
 	     SSB_SPROM8_TEMPDELTA_HYSTERESIS_SHIFT);
 }
 
+static void bcma_sprom_unpack_rxgains(struct ssb_sprom_rxgains *out,
+				      int chain, u8 packed)
+{
+	out->trelnabyp[chain] = (packed >> 7) & 0x01;
+	out->triso[chain]     = (packed >> 3) & 0x0f;
+	out->elnagain[chain]  =  packed       & 0x07;
+}
+
+static void bcma_sprom_extract_r11(struct bcma_bus *bus, const u16 *sprom)
+{
+	static const u16 pwr_info_offset[] = {
+		SSB_SPROM11_PWR_INFO_CORE0,
+		SSB_SPROM11_PWR_INFO_CORE1,
+		SSB_SPROM11_PWR_INFO_CORE2,
+	};
+	u16 v;
+	int i, j;
+
+	BUILD_BUG_ON(ARRAY_SIZE(pwr_info_offset) >
+		     ARRAY_SIZE(bus->sprom.core_pwr_info));
+
+	/* Header: rev-11 offsets, rev-8 masks/shifts. */
+	for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+		v = sprom[SPOFF(SSB_SPROM11_IL0MAC) + i];
+		*(((__be16 *)bus->sprom.il0mac) + i) = cpu_to_be16(v);
+	}
+
+	SPEX(board_rev, SSB_SPROM8_BOARDREV, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(board_type, SSB_SPROM1_SPID, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(boardflags_lo, SSB_SPROM8_BFLLO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(boardflags_hi, SSB_SPROM8_BFLHI, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(boardflags2_lo, SSB_SPROM8_BFL2LO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(boardflags2_hi, SSB_SPROM8_BFL2HI, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(boardflags3, SSB_SPROM11_BFL3, ~0, 0);
+
+	SPEX(country_code, SSB_SPROM11_CCODE, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(gpio0, SSB_SPROM11_GPIOA, SSB_SPROM8_GPIOA_P0, 0);
+	SPEX(gpio1, SSB_SPROM11_GPIOA, SSB_SPROM8_GPIOA_P1,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_GPIOA_P1_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(gpio2, SSB_SPROM11_GPIOB, SSB_SPROM8_GPIOB_P2, 0);
+	SPEX(gpio3, SSB_SPROM11_GPIOB, SSB_SPROM8_GPIOB_P3,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_GPIOB_P3_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(ant_available_a, SSB_SPROM11_ANTAVAIL, SSB_SPROM8_ANTAVAIL_A,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_ANTAVAIL_A_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(ant_available_bg, SSB_SPROM11_ANTAVAIL, SSB_SPROM8_ANTAVAIL_BG,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_ANTAVAIL_BG_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(txchain, SSB_SPROM11_TXRXC, SSB_SPROM8_TXRXC_TXCHAIN,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_TXRXC_TXCHAIN_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(rxchain, SSB_SPROM11_TXRXC, SSB_SPROM8_TXRXC_RXCHAIN,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_TXRXC_RXCHAIN_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(antswitch, SSB_SPROM11_TXRXC, SSB_SPROM8_TXRXC_SWITCH,
+	     SSB_SPROM8_TXRXC_SWITCH_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(subband5gver, SSB_SPROM11_SUBBAND5GVER, 0x00ff, 0);
+
+	/* FEM / PA control block. */
+	SPEX(tssiposslope2g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG1,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_TSSIPOSSLOPE, 0);
+	SPEX(epagain2g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG1, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_EPAGAIN,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_EPAGAIN_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(pdgain2g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG1, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PDGAIN,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PDGAIN_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(tworangetssi2g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG1,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_TWORANGETSSI, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_TWORANGETSSI_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(papdcap2g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG1, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PAPDCAP,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PAPDCAP_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(femctrl, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG1, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CTRL,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CTRL_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(tssiposslope5g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG2,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_TSSIPOSSLOPE, 0);
+	SPEX(epagain5g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG2, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_EPAGAIN,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_EPAGAIN_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(pdgain5g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG2, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PDGAIN,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PDGAIN_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(tworangetssi5g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG2,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_TWORANGETSSI, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_TWORANGETSSI_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(papdcap5g, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG2, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PAPDCAP,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_PAPDCAP_SHIFT);
+	SPEX(gainctrlsph, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_CFG2,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_FEM_GAINCTRLSPH, SSB_SPROM11_FEM_GAINCTRLSPH_SHIFT);
+
+	/* TSSI floor. */
+	SPEX(tssifloor2g, SSB_SPROM11_TSSIFLOOR_2G,
+	     SSB_SPROM11_TSSIFLOOR_MASK, 0);
+	for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
+		SPEX(tssifloor5g[i], SSB_SPROM11_TSSIFLOOR_5GL + i * 2,
+		     SSB_SPROM11_TSSIFLOOR_MASK, 0);
+
+	/* Per-chain power info. */
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(pwr_info_offset); i++) {
+		struct ssb_sprom_core_pwr_info *p = &bus->sprom.core_pwr_info[i];
+		u16 base = pwr_info_offset[i];
+
+		p->maxp2ga = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_MAXP2GA)] & 0xff;
+
+		for (j = 0; j < 3; j++)
+			p->pa2ga[j] = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_PA2GA) + j];
+
+		v = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_MAXP5GA)];
+		p->maxp5ga[0] = v & 0xff;
+		p->maxp5ga[1] = (v >> 8) & 0xff;
+		v = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_MAXP5GA) + 1];
+		p->maxp5ga[2] = v & 0xff;
+		p->maxp5ga[3] = (v >> 8) & 0xff;
+
+		for (j = 0; j < 12; j++)
+			p->pa5ga[j] = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_PA5GA) + j];
+
+		v = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_RXGAINS0)];
+		bcma_sprom_unpack_rxgains(&bus->sprom.rxgains_5gm, i,  v       & 0xff);
+		bcma_sprom_unpack_rxgains(&bus->sprom.rxgains_5gh, i, (v >> 8) & 0xff);
+		v = sprom[SPOFF(base + SSB_SPROM11_PWR_RXGAINS1)];
+		bcma_sprom_unpack_rxgains(&bus->sprom.rxgains_2g,  i,  v       & 0xff);
+		bcma_sprom_unpack_rxgains(&bus->sprom.rxgains_5gl, i, (v >> 8) & 0xff);
+	}
+
+	SPEX(rpcal2g,    SSB_SPROM11_RPCAL_2G,  ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(rpcal5gb[0], SSB_SPROM11_RPCAL_5GL, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(rpcal5gb[1], SSB_SPROM11_RPCAL_5GM, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(rpcal5gb[2], SSB_SPROM11_RPCAL_5GH, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(rpcal5gb[3], SSB_SPROM11_RPCAL_5GU, ~0, 0);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+		bus->sprom.pdoffset40ma[i] =
+			sprom[SPOFF(SSB_SPROM11_PDOFFSET40MA) + i];
+		bus->sprom.pdoffset80ma[i] =
+			sprom[SPOFF(SSB_SPROM11_PDOFFSET80MA) + i];
+	}
+
+	SPEX(cckbw202gpo,           SSB_SPROM11_CCKBW202GPO,           ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(cckbw20ul2gpo,         SSB_SPROM11_CCKBW20UL2GPO,         ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw202gpo,         SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW202GPO,           ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw402gpo,         SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW402GPO,           ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(dot11agofdmhrbw202gpo, SSB_SPROM11_DOT11AGOFDMHRBW202GPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(ofdmlrbw202gpo,        SSB_SPROM11_OFDMLRBW202GPO,        ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw205glpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW205GLPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw405glpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW405GLPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw805glpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW805GLPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw205gmpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW205GMPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw405gmpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW405GMPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw805gmpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW805GMPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw205ghpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW205GHPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw405ghpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW405GHPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX32(mcsbw805ghpo,        SSB_SPROM11_MCSBW805GHPO,          ~0, 0);
+
+	SPEX(mcslr5gpo[0],          SSB_SPROM11_MCSLR5GLPO,            ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(mcslr5gpo[1],          SSB_SPROM11_MCSLR5GMPO,            ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(mcslr5gpo[2],          SSB_SPROM11_MCSLR5GHPO,            ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in40hrpo,          SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN40HRPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in40lrpo,          SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN40LRPO,          ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in80and160hr5gpo[0], SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN80AND160HR5GLPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in80and160hr5gpo[1], SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN80AND160HR5GMPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in80and160hr5gpo[2], SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN80AND160HR5GHPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in80and160lr5gpo[0], SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN80AND160LR5GLPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in80and160lr5gpo[1], SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN80AND160LR5GMPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb20in80and160lr5gpo[2], SSB_SPROM11_SB20IN80AND160LR5GHPO, ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb40and80hr5gpo[0],    SSB_SPROM11_SB40AND80HR5GLPO,      ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb40and80hr5gpo[1],    SSB_SPROM11_SB40AND80HR5GMPO,      ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb40and80hr5gpo[2],    SSB_SPROM11_SB40AND80HR5GHPO,      ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb40and80lr5gpo[0],    SSB_SPROM11_SB40AND80LR5GLPO,      ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb40and80lr5gpo[1],    SSB_SPROM11_SB40AND80LR5GMPO,      ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(sb40and80lr5gpo[2],    SSB_SPROM11_SB40AND80LR5GHPO,      ~0, 0);
+	SPEX(dot11agduphrpo,        SSB_SPROM11_DOT11AGDUPHRPO,        ~0, 0);
